//? Title: Integrate a random intercept in a count model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare Laplace and adaptive Gauss–Hermite fits

Eight production lines record defect counts at four settings. The
Poisson model includes a common setting coefficient and one Gaussian
random intercept per line. A log exposure offset accounts for the
different inspection lengths.

The fitter integrates each line's likelihood over its random
intercept. Laplace uses the conditional mode and curvature. Adaptive
Gauss–Hermite quadrature uses additional points around that same
mode.

```{cpp, id=integrated-defect-likelihood, file="figures/likelihood_glmm_quadrature.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/likelihood_glmm.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    const std::vector<std::size_t> count{
        0,1,1,1, 1,2,2,3, 2,3,3,4, 3,4,7,6, 0,0,1,2, 5,8,13,9, 1,1,2,2, 0,2,4,3};
    const double setting[]{-1,-.4,.35,1}, exposure[]{.8,1,1.2,1.5};
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> x(count.size(), 2, 1);
    std::vector<std::size_t> lines;
    stats::LikelihoodGLMMOptions options{.family = stats::GLMMFamily::Poisson};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count.size(); ++i) {
        x(i, 1) = setting[i % 4];
        options.offset.push_back(std::log(exposure[i % 4]));
        lines.push_back(i / 4);
    }

    std::vector<double> points, likelihood;
    std::println("| Quadrature points | Random-intercept SD | Log likelihood |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const std::size_t q : {1,5,9,15,31}) {
        options.quadrature_points = q;
        const auto fit = stats::fit_likelihood_glmm(x, count, lines, options);
        if (fit.status != stats::LikelihoodGLMMStatus::Converged)
            throw std::runtime_error("Expected an interior random-intercept fit");
        std::println("| {} | {:.5f} | {:.8f} |", q, fit.random_intercept_sd, fit.log_likelihood);
        points.push_back(q);
        likelihood.push_back(fit.log_likelihood);
    }
    std::vector<double> error;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i + 1 < points.size(); ++i)
        error.push_back(std::log10(std::abs(likelihood[i] - likelihood.back())));
    points.pop_back();
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create()
        .line(points, error, "", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .points(points, error, "", {29, 78, 216}, 5)
        .title("Sensitivity to quadrature order").x_label("Quadrature points")
        .y_label("Log10 absolute difference from the 31-point fit")
        .size(740, 420).save_svg("build/likelihood_glmm_quadrature.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Quadrature points | Random-intercept SD | Log likelihood |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.73281 | -56.99228908 |
| 5 | 0.73625 | -56.96994589 |
| 9 | 0.73647 | -56.96901581 |
| 15 | 0.73647 | -56.96900243 |
| 31 | 0.73647 | -56.96900244 |
\image(figures/likelihood_glmm_quadrature.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The difference from the 31-point log likelihood falls to about one hundred-millionth by 15 points.)
\alttext(On a base-10 log scale, the difference falls from around minus 1.6 at one point to minus 8 at 15 points.)

Each row refits the model at that quadrature order. Here the estimated
random-intercept SD changes little past five points, while the
log likelihood keeps changing at smaller scales.

[Liu and Pierce, A note on Gauss–Hermite quadrature|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/81.3.624]
